  "scenes": [
    {
      "narration": "Line up the price of everyday things in nineteen seventy against today, and the numbers are almost comical.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "hook",
          "art": "priceTag",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "clock"
          ],
          "lines": [
            "1970",
            "vs today."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s1"
    },
    {
      "narration": "A single nineteen seventy dollar now buys what about eight dollars buys today.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "thennow",
          "kicker": "The dollar",
          "thenLabel": "1970",
          "thenValue": "$1",
          "nowLabel": "Today",
          "nowValue": "~$8",
          "sub": "same buying power"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s2"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Inflation has steadily eroded the dollar for over fifty years.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-inflation",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · inflation"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s3"
    },
    {
      "narration": "At typical rates, prices roughly double every eighteen years, again and again.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "priceTag",
          "big": "2× / 18 yrs",
          "label": "how fast prices double",
          "props": [
            "chartUp",
            "clock"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s4"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The CPI has tracked that climb the entire time.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-cpi",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · consumer price index"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s5"
    },
    {
      "narration": "But not everything rose evenly. Home prices jumped around eighteen times while real wages stayed almost flat.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "home",
          "big": "18× vs flat",
          "label": "home prices vs real wages",
          "props": [
            "home",
            "chartDown"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s6"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Real wages measure what your pay can actually buy.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-realwages",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · real wages"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s7"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Which is exactly why a single income stretched so much further back then.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "ink",
          "line": "One income was enough",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "home"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s8"
    },
    {
      "narration": "One income used to buy a house and raise a family; now it barely covers the rent.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "red",
          "line": "One income, then vs now",
          "props": [
            "home",
            "chartDown"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s9"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The trick is comparing real values, adjusted for inflation.",
